.TH BT9P 4
.SH NAME
bt9p, btmock \- Bluetooth host, serving the controller as /net/bt
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bt9p
[
.B -D
] [
.B -m
.I mountpoint
] [
.B -k
.I keyfile
] [
.B -f
.I factotum
]
.B -t
.I transport
.PP
.B btmock
[
.B -a
.I addr
] [
.B -n
.I 'addr class rssi name'
] ... [
.B -s
] [
.B -t
.I ms
] [
.I path
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Bt9p
is the Bluetooth host: the one program that owns a controller, speaks
HCI to it through
.IR bthci (2),
and puts it behind files. It opens
.I transport
-- a file such as the board's
.BR /dev/eia0 ,
or a dial string such as
.BR tcp!host!port ,
anything with a
.B !
in it is dialled -- and speaks H4 over it. A file transport with a
.B ctl
file beside it
.RB ( /dev/eia0ctl )
is a serial port, and gets hardware flow control turned on
.RB ( m1 ):
the Broadcom controller does not transmit until its CTS is asserted.
Nothing in the kernel knows the word Bluetooth; the controller's power line is a pin in
.B #G
.RB ( os/bcm2837/devgpio.c ,
pin 128 on a Pi 3B+) and its UART is a port in
.IR eia (3).
.PP
The tree is served into
.I mountpoint
(default
.BR /net ,
union-mounted after, so that
.B /net/bt
appears beside
.B /net/tcp
and
.BR /net/ether0 ).
.B -D
traces every command and event to standard error.
.SH FILESYSTEM
.TF status
.TP
.B addr
Read-only: the controller's BD_ADDR,
.BR b8:27:eb:5a:6b:7c ,
one line. An error until the controller is up.
.TP
.B status
Read-only, one field per line:
.B up
0 or 1; and when up,
.BR addr ,
.BR name ,
a version line
.RB ( "hci 4.2 lmp 4.2 manufacturer 15 (Broadcom)" ),
.BR class ,
.BR discoverable ,
.BR connectable ;
always
.B transport
(what was opened),
.B scans
(inquiries run),
.B events dropped
(events nobody was reading);
.B controller gone
if the transport has died;
.B hci held
while the raw file is open.
.TP
.B ctl
Write-only. Verbs that talk to the controller wait for its answer
and fail with the HCI status name if it refuses; an unknown verb, a
malformed one, or one for a milestone not yet built is an error,
never silence.
.RS
.TP
.B up
Reset the controller and learn who it is: local version, BD_ADDR,
name; set the event mask; apply the scan enable. Fails with
.B no controller: ...
if nothing answers the reset.
.TP
.B down
Disable page and inquiry scan and mark the controller down.
.TP
.B reset
HCI_Reset; the controller forgets its name and scan settings, and is
down.
.TP
.BI name " string"
Write_Local_Name; the rest of the line is the name.
.TP
.BI class " hex"
Write_Class_Of_Device.
.TP
.BI bdaddr " xx:xx:xx:xx:xx:xx"
Broadcom's Write_BD_ADDR, then Read_BD_ADDR to record what took.
Refused unless the controller says it is Broadcom's or Cypress's,
because the same opcode means something else elsewhere. The CYW43455's
patch firmware leaves the address at the patch's default, so a board
that wants its own sets it here; what it should be is the boot
script's decision, not this program's.
.TP
.BR discoverable " on|off"
.TP
.BR connectable " on|off"
Write_Scan_Enable, inquiry scan and page scan respectively.
.TP
.BI scan " seconds"
How long the next inquiry runs, 1 to 61 (default 10).
.TP
.BR iocap " none|display|yesno|keyboard"
What we tell peers we can do about pairing (default
.BR none ,
so Secure Simple Pairing is Just Works and nothing prompts).
.TP
.BR pairable " on|off"
Whether a peer may start a pairing we did not ask for (default
.BR off :
refused, with
.B "failed ... pairing not allowed"
on
.BR pair ).
Pairing on a connection we made is always allowed.
.TP
.BI pair " addr"
Pair with a classic peer for its own sake: a link is made to it,
authenticated and encrypted -- pairing on the way when there is no
key, under
.B iocap
and
.B pairable
as any pairing -- the key kept, and the link let go. The write returns
when that is done, or fails saying why. An LE peripheral pairs at its
first
.B connect
instead.
.TP
.BI forget " addr"
Delete the link key for
.IR addr ,
from factotum and from the keys file.
.TP
.BI firmware " path"
Name a Broadcom
.B .hcd
patch file; the next
.B up
uploads it -- Download_Minidriver, every record, Launch_RAM -- then
resets and asks the controller who it is now. On the board the file
is
.BR /n/dos/firmware/BCM4345C0.hcd ,
on the card and never in the tree.
.B status
reports how many records went, or that the upload failed.
.TP
.BI baud " rate"
Update_UART_Baud_Rate to the controller, at the old rate, then
.BI b rate
to the transport's own ctl file
.RB ( /dev/eia0ctl
for
.BR /dev/eia0 );
refused for a transport that has none.
.RE
.TP
.B scan
Read-only, one reader at a time. The first read starts an inquiry;
each read returns the devices whose lines are ready, one per line,
.IR "addr class rssi name" ,
class in hex, rssi in dBm. A device's line is written once its name
is known: from its extended inquiry response, or from a Remote Name
Request made for it after the inquiry, or
.B -
if it will not say. EOF after the last. Closing the file early
cancels the inquiry. A second open while one runs fails with
.BR "scan in progress" .
.TP
.B lescan
The same for Bluetooth Low Energy: an active scan for the
.B scan
time, one line per device heard,
.IR "addr public|random rssi name" ,
the name from its advertising data or
.BR - ;
EOF when the time is up.
.TP
.B event
Read-only. The HCI event stream as text, one event per line,
.BR "event 0x0e 01 03 0c 00" ,
for as long as the file is held open; what
.I btmon
shows. Each reader has its own queue, bounded at a thousand lines.
Owner-only.
.TP
.B hci
Read/write, exclusive. Raw H4 packets: a write is a packet with its
indicator byte, a read returns the next packet the controller sent.
While it is held the stack has lent the controller to the holder,
and
.B ctl
verbs that would issue commands are refused. For bring-up and tests.
Owner-only.
.TP
.B pair
Read-only stream of pairing prompts while held open:
.BI "confirm " "addr number"
(numeric comparison),
.BI "passkey " "addr number"
(show it to the peer),
.BI "passkey? " addr
(the peer showed one),
.BI "paired " addrR,
.BI "failed " "addr why"R.
Written to answer:
.BI "yes " addrR,
.BI "no " addrR,
.BI "passkey " "addr digits"R.
A confirmation that arrives with nobody holding the file is refused.
Owner-only.
.TP
.B clone
Opening it makes a new conversation
.I N
and the open file is
.IR N 's
.BR ctl :
reading gives
.IR N .
The conversation lives while any of its files is open and no longer;
.IR dial (2)
holds the clone open for the life of the connection, and so should
anything else.
.TP
.BI N /ctl
Write-only verbs, as
.BR /net/tcp 's:
.RS
.TP
.BI connect " addr" ! port
Connect to
.I port
on the device at
.IR addr ,
over an ACL link made if there is not one; the write returns when the
connection is open, or fails saying why -- a port the peer does not
serve, a device that does not answer its page, a peer that never
answers (fifteen seconds).
.I port
is one of:
.RS
.TP
.I psm
An L2CAP channel; decimal or 0x hex, odd.
.TP
.BI rfcomm n
A serial port: RFCOMM server channel
.IR n ,
1 to 30, on the link's one multiplexer, which is made for the first
serial port to a device and taken down after the last.
.TP
.B spp
A serial port whose channel the peer's SDP record names: the Serial
Port Profile record is asked for and its RFCOMM channel used. Fails
with
.I no serial port offered
if there is none.
.TP
.B gatt
An LE link to the peer, encrypted first (below), with the Attribute
Protocol on
.BR data :
one ATT PDU per write, and each PDU the peer sends is one read.
.TP
.B hid
An LE link, encrypted, then the peer's HID service found with GATT:
the device is put in boot protocol if it has one and its input
reports subscribed to, and each report the device notifies is one
read of
.BR data .
A boot mouse report is three bytes, buttons then dx then dy; a boot
keyboard report is eight. A device with no boot report has its Report
Map read and parsed
.RI ( hid (2)),
and each of its reports is handed on in the boot layout -- buttons,
dx, dy, wheel -- so the reader never sees the map. The conversation's
.B status
says which:
.BR "Connected boot-mouse" ,
.BR boot-keyboard ,
.B mouse
(a report-protocol mouse, translated) or
.B report
(a map describing no mouse; reports as they came).
.IR bthid (1)
is the reader that makes a pointer of them. Writes are refused.
.PP
An LE link is encrypted before either port is used: with the long-term
key factotum holds for the peer
.RB ( "proto=btltk" ),
or, when it has none and
.B pairable
is on, by pairing -- Just Works, legacy pairing; the key the device
gives is stored as
.B btlink
keys are, in factotum and in the keys file, and offered next time.
A device that will only pair with LE Secure Connections is refused
and says so. The peer's address type is what
.B lescan
heard for it, or, unheard, what the address itself says -- so scan
first for a device with a resolvable private address.
.RE
.TP
.BI announce " port"
Serve
.I port
on this conversation: peers that connect to it appear through
.BR listen .
The
.BI * ! port
form
.IR announce (2)
writes for
.BI bt!*! port
is taken too, and a local half other than
.B *
or this controller's address is refused. For
.BI rfcomm n
and
.B spp
-- the latter the lowest free channel -- a Serial Port Profile record
naming the channel is offered over SDP for as long as the conversation
is announced, which is how a peer's
.B spp
finds it. SDP itself (PSM 1) is always answered while the controller is
up; a peer asking what we offer gets the list, empty or not.
.TP
.B hangup
Disconnect the channel.
.RE
.TP
.BI N /data
L2CAP: one SDU per write and one per read, a whole frame each; a write
larger than the peer's MTU fails saying so. RFCOMM: a byte stream, as
a serial port is -- a read returns what has arrived, up to its count,
and a write of any length is framed and paced by the peer's credits.
Reads return EOF once the connection has gone.
.TP
.BI N /status
.BR Connected ,
.BR Connecting ,
.BR Listen ,
.BR Closed ,
or
.BI "Hangup " why\fR.
.TP
.BI N /local
.RI "" addr ! port
of this end;
.I port
as it was given, so a serial port reads
.BI rfcomm n
(an
.B spp
connect reads the channel SDP found).
.TP
.BI N /remote
The peer's.
.TP
.BI N /listen
On an announced conversation: the open blocks until a peer connects
to the PSM, and then reading gives the accepted conversation's number
-- which is how
.IR dial (2)'s
.I listen
works. The accepted conversation is held until the listen file is
closed.
.PP
Because the shape is
.BR /net/tcp 's,
.IR dial (2)
works unchanged:
.BR "dial(""bt!b8:27:eb:11:22:33!17"")" ,
.BR "announce(""bt!*!17"")" ,
and
.IR ndb/cs (8)
translates
.B bt!
addresses to
.B /net/bt/clone
because that file exists.
.SH KEYS
LE peripherals add a third protocol:
.IP
.B "key proto=btltk addr=<peer> type=<0|1> ediv=<n> rand=<16 hex> !ltk=<32 hex>"
.PP
the long-term key an LE pairing produced, with the EDIV and Rand the
controller must be given to start encryption with it, and the peer's
address type. Stored and forgotten as the others are.
Keys are held the way WiFi's are. factotum is the only source of
secrets: a peer's Link Key Request is answered from
.BR "proto=btlink addr=" IpeerR,
a PIN Code Request from
.BR "proto=btpin addr=" IpeerR
and then
.B proto=btpin
alone -- the protocol modules are
.B auth/proto/btlink
and
.BR auth/proto/btpin .
A link key the controller reports at the end of a pairing is written
to factotum's ctl as
.IP
.EX
key proto=btlink addr=94:bb:43:44:61:04 type=4 !key=<32 hex digits>
.EE
.PP
and, given
.BR -k ,
appended to
.I keyfile
in the same line; at start every
.B key
line in
.I keyfile
is written to factotum, one write each, so a board loads its pairings
from the card as it loads its WiFi keys. Legacy PINs are pre-shared
and go in the same file:
.IP
.EX
key proto=btpin addr=00:1f:20:aa:bb:cc !pin=0000
key proto=btpin !pin=1234
.EE
.PP
.B -f
names factotum's directory (default
.BR /mnt/factotum ).
.B "cat /mnt/factotum/ctl"
lists whom the machine is paired with, keys elided.
.PP
See
.B docs/BLUETOOTH.md
for the whole tree and the order it arrives in.
.SH BTMOCK
.I Btmock
is a fake controller as a file, served through
.IR file2chan
at
.I path
(default
.BR /chan/btmock ).
Bytes written are H4 commands, bytes read are the controller's
answers, so
.B "bt9p -t /chan/btmock"
runs with no radio and no kernel.
.B -a
sets its address;
.B -n
adds a device an inquiry finds, repeatable; a fifth word
.BI pin= NNNN
or
.B ssp
makes it demand pairing before it connects, by that PIN or by numeric
comparison of 123456;
.B -s
makes it stingy with command credits, refunding them on the tick;
.B -t
sets the tick in milliseconds (default 200), one inquiry result per
tick. Every nearby device answers L2CAP connections to PSM 0x1001 by
echoing what it is sent, offers a serial port on RFCOMM channel 1 that
echoes too, and says so in an SDP record. A second file,
.IB path ctl\fR,
is the far side's telephone: writing
.BI "call " "addr port text"
makes the device at
.I addr
connect to the host and send
.I text
on
.I port
-- a PSM, or
.BI rfcomm n
-- and reading it lists what the peers received, one
.BI "recv " "addr port text"
per line.
.SH EXAMPLES
On the board:
.IP
.EX
bind -a '#t' /dev
echo 1 > '#G/gpio/128/level'
bt9p -t /dev/eia0 -k /n/dos/btkeys
echo up > /net/bt/ctl
cat /net/bt/addr
cat /net/bt/scan
.EE
.PP
Anywhere:
.IP
.EX
btmock -n '94:bb:43:44:61:04 0x1c010c -61 hephaestus' &
bt9p -t /chan/btmock
echo up > /net/bt/ctl
cat /net/bt/status
.EE
.SH AUDIT
When
.B /mnt/audit/log
is in the namespace (see
.IR auditfs (4)),
one record goes there for each thing that changes whom the machine
trusts or talks to:
.BR up ,
.BR down ,
.BI "paired peer=" addr " iocap=" modeR,
.BI "forget peer=" addrR,
and
.BI "connect peer=" addr " port=" port " incoming|outgoing"R.
Without it nothing is recorded and nothing fails: a radio that cannot
come up for want of a log is a worse outcome than an unlogged radio.
The pairing prompts and refusals are on
.BR event ,
which is the transcript, not the record.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/bt9p.b
.br
.B /appl/cmd/btmock.b
.br
.B /appl/lib/bthci.b
.br
.B /appl/lib/btmock.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR eia (3),
.IR dial (2),
.B docs/BLUETOOTH.md
.SH BUGS
On a Pi 3B+ the CYW43455 has answered
.BR up ,
taken its firmware, found a peer by inquiry and carried an L2CAP
conversation each way; pairing and
.B baud
above 115200 have run against
.I btmock
only. The address after the firmware is the patch's default, not the
board's; there is no verb yet to set it.
Started from the network console it outlives the session and keeps the
port, and the next instance's Reset is answered to the first one:
kill it before starting another.
